AB  - obstructive sleep apnea is a life-threatening disease characterized by upper airway narrowing or obstruction. The diagnosis is difficult, costly, and time-consuming. Many individuals with obstructive sleep apnea do not apply for a diagnosis or are unaware of their disease. This study aimed to develop a practical, fast, and reliable diagnostic system for early diagnosis and treatment of obstructive sleep apnea. For the first time, features were extracted from flow-volume curves obtained using a Pulmonary Function Test (PFT), and an Artificial Intelligence (artificial intelligence)-based algorithm was developed to diagnose obstructive sleep apnea. Spearman correlation coefficients determined the degree of influence of the features in determining obstructive sleep apnea. Several models were created using different features and artificial intelligence according to their effect levels. The models obtained by hyperparameter optimization and cross-validation were tested with unseen data, and their performance was evaluated using seven different criteria. Using only five features extracted from the flow-volume curve (TLC/PIF, PIF/PEF, TLC/FIF50, TLC/FIF25, and FIF25/FEF25), obstructive sleep apnea was diagnosed with 97.1% accuracy using the Neural Network (NN) algorithm. The results showed that obstructive sleep apnea can be diagnosed quickly and reliably using PFT available at every hospital. The features extracted from the flow-volume curve could be used as biomarkers for diagnosing obstructive sleep apnea. The proposed method can be adapted to PC-based spirometry devices without additional hardware developments. This is a significant innovation in both literature and practice. This method will enable early diagnosis for patients and many people unaware of their disease. This will shed light on several future studies.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=IEEE Xplore; Groups=G3; G5; G7; CitationKey=eris2025huge
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
ID  - fang2025assessmentsimulate
AU  - Fang, Lucheng
AU  - Cai, Jie
AU  - Huang, Zilin
AU  - Tuohuti, Aikebaier
AU  - Chen, Xiong
TI  - Assessment of simulated snoring sounds with artificial intelligence for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea
PY  - 2025
T2  - Sleep Medicine
JO  - Sleep Medicine
DO  - 10.1016/j.sleep.2024.11.018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sleep.2024.11.018
PB  - Elsevier
KW  - deep learning
KW  - machine learning
KW  - Diagnosis
KW  - AHI estimation
KW  - Snoring / sleep audio
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Elsevier
AB  - Background Performing simulated snoring (SS) is a commonly used method to evaluate the source of snoring in obstructive sleep apnea. SS sounds is considered as a potential biomarker for obstructive sleep apnea. SS sounds can be easily recorded, which is a cost-effective method for prescreening purposes. Objective This study aimed to validate the performance of artificial intelligence (artificial intelligence) models using SS sounds for obstructive sleep apnea diagnosis. Methods All participants underwent full-night polysomnography monitoring at the sleep center. SS sounds of the participants were recorded during the laryngoscopy procedure. The audio data were processed via Python, and relevant features were extracted. obstructive sleep apnea diagnostic models were developed using three machine learning (ML) algorithms and one deep learning (DL) algorithm. The diagnostic performance was evaluated by multiple indicators. Results A total of 465 participants were included. For the support vector machine algorithm, the accuracy values at apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.914, 0.887, and 0.807, respectively. For the K-nearest neighbor algorithm, the accuracy values at AHI levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.896, 0.872, and 0.756, respectively. For the random forest algorithm, the accuracy values at AHI levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.905, 0.881, and 0.804, respectively. For the audio spectrogram transformer algorithm, the accuracy values at AHI levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.926, 0.887, and 0.830, respectively. Conclusions Our study demonstrates that DL models can effectively screen and identify obstructive sleep apnea with commendable performance. In addition, the identification ability of the DL models was better than that of any of the ML models.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=Elsevier; Groups=G3; CitationKey=fang2025assessmentsimulate
